--- !u!114 &5529137537738272508
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2385402032622750858}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Name: Evelyn Ventress\r\nAge: 43\r\n\nStrengths:\r\nIntelligence\r\nRational\r\nPhysical
    Strength\r\n\r\nWeakness:\r\nFear of being alone forever\r\nFear of being ostracized\r\nFear
    of the unknown\r\n\nGoal Room: Armory\nFear Room: Dungeon\n\nCharacter Story
    on Next Page\n\n\n\tEvelyn Ventress is a psychologist with the Alchemilla Clinic.
    Her features are sharp. The eyes are seemingly permanently wide; so brown that
    it barely distinguishes itself from the black of her pupil. When Evelyn speaks
    to someone, she rarely breaks eye contact, only ever to study the other nuances
    of a face. Her nose is small and the bridge is constantly wrinkled as she flips
    through the pages of a book or sorts through the files on her desk. Her long
    flat lips are pale. She may wear makeup but it\u2019s hard for anyone to tell
    for sure. Her clothes are plain and she doesn\u2019t wear rings or jewelry of
    any kind. She is deceptively muscular. Her lab coat conceals the definition in
    her arms. The people she works with never see her without it. \r\n\tShe starts
    every morning with a workout after turning the extra bedroom into a personal
    gym. She listens to the news or a relevant lecture while doing cardio, only pausing
    to check metrics from the smart watch on her wrist. Her breakfast varies but
    is always balanced. She showers quickly, changes, and heads off to work in an
    electric compact car. She doesn\u2019t work with patients anymore. Evelyn put
    in her time in that department and allows that work to be done by her graduate
    students. All of her efforts are put towards the research now. Interpreting and
    sorting the data that comes from her varies studies. The walls of her office
    are covered in various accolades and recognitions. They wouldn\u2019t be on display
    if they didn\u2019t come framed. When five pm comes, she gathers her things and
    tries to participate in friendly conversation with her team. It feels forced
    for everyone involved. \r\n\tShe drives home to get ready. She owns only two
    things that would be seen as passable for a date. Looking at her closet, she
    criticizes herself for her apathy toward it all. The next hour is spent in front
    of the mirror, checking every part of herself to a metric she\u2019s not sure
    she understands. Looking down at the dainty rose gold watch, light on her wrist
    compared to the brick she spends her days with, she knows she has to leave. Evelyn
    takes one more look at herself in the mirror, takes a deep breath, and smiles
    to herself as genuinely as she can muster before starting to leave. She stops
    on her way out, grabbing a small pocket knife on a side table in the vestibule.
    \r\n\tThis was her fifth first date in three months. She had begun to get tired
    of the same small talk. This night something was different. Gene wore an expertly
    tailored suit and he never seemed to stop smiling. She found it easy to talk
    to him. He picked up a lot of the slack, talking when she didn\u2019t want to.
    His eyes would dart fast every which way when he talked eventually landing back
    on hers when he got to the punchline or resolved a story. She didn\u2019t think
    he was particularly funny but she was able to fake her way through it convincingly,
    which was enough for her. He said her name, \u201CEvelyn\u201D, with a knowingness
    that was immediately attention grabbing. She couldn\u2019t figure out if it was
    attractive or creepy.  He kept fiddling with his sleeves the entire night, itching
    at the place where his shirt met his wrist. After what she thought was an appropriate
    amount of time, she made some excuse to get home early, making an effort to get
    his number. \r\n\tRight before they were about to go separate ways in the parking
    lot. Gene stopped her. He pulled out an envelope from his back pocket and gave
    it to her. \u201CThis might interest you,\u201D he said. Before Evelyn could
    ask any questions, he walked away. She never saw him drive away. He just walked
    into the blackness of the night. She took the letter home and stared at it on
    the kitchen table. She felt a weird aura coming from it. After staring at it
    for what seemed like an eternity, she pushed aside her hesitation as irrational
    and open the paper. The last thing Evelyn remembers before waking up is reading
    the words: REDACTED"
