--- !u!114 &6948747114901222014
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 7486595461266312976}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Name: Iain Schweblin\r\nAge: 46\r\n\nStrengths:\r\nCreative\r\nSocially
    Capable\r\nStrong will\r\n\r\nWeakness:\r\nFear of crowds\r\nFear of loss\r\nFear
    of losing his mind\r\n\nGoal Room: Ritual Room\nFear Room: Ghost Room\n\nCharacter
    Story on Next Page\n\n\n\tIain Schewblin is a middle-aged man. He has a long
    face that leads down to a thin, but well trimmed beard. He wears thick, tortoise
    shell glasses, hooked on large ears. Started balding early so, to save himself
    the trouble, he went bald at the age of 26. He hasn\u2019t grown it out since.
    \r\n\tIain Schweblin is a father of two. When people ask him what he does, that\u2019s
    what he says. If he were to answer the question they were actually asking, he\u2019d
    say he\u2019s  a writer. That is how he makes his money and that is what drove
    him to a small town in Wyoming that would be labeled as nowhere, if anyone was
    there to declare it. Iain certainly never would. He loved his life on the empty
    plains. It was worth the 40-minute drive to take his kids to school. It was worth
    the utility charges. It was all worth it to be away from it all.\r\n\tMr. Schweblin
    was not a shut-in, at least not in the traditional sense. He got along with people
    just fine and had no problem making conversation at the bank or the grocery store.
    He enjoyed any chance he had to stretch that muscle. Iain tried to take the kids
    into Cheyenne as often as he could, although they rarely complained. There wasn\u2019t
    much for kids of fifteen and thirteen to do in that town, but they always found
    ways to spend their time. He tried to give the kids space on those trips. His
    childhood was filled with days spent wandering unsupervised, and he convinced
    himself this is what gave him his creative edge. Was he giving them some freedom?
    Was he just a lazy parent? He hadn\u2019t decided. All he knew was that he didn\u2019t
    want to trap them on that vast, empty plain. He just couldn\u2019t handle  crowds.
    \r\n\tThey met in college. She was nineteen. He was twenty. She was in his novel
    writing class. Before that meeting, girls weren\u2019t something Iain thought
    about. At least, that\u2019s what he told his friends when they asked about his
    embarrassing lack of \u201Cgame,\u201D as they called it. The truth was he did
    think about it, probably too much. He told himself that eventually he\u2019d
    have the guts to walk up to someone like her and win her over with his quick
    wit. But it was so much easier to live in his head where the risk wasn\u2019t
    real.\r\n\tShe wrote her name on a piece of paper, dropped it on his desk, and
    walked out of the class. Grace Adrian. On the other side, she\u2019d written
    her number, looping the middle of her threes.They had talked maybe two times
    during class projects. She laughed at his jokes, but he didn\u2019t think much
    of it. But suddenly all he did was think about it. He spent the day staring at
    that piece of paper, wondering what to do, what to say.  After making himself 
    nearly crazy, he picked up the phone and dialed. \r\n\tTheirs was a story like
    that of many college sweethearts. She made his writing better, letting him know
    when the pieces didn\u2019t fit together or when his drive to sound smart got
    in the way of his better judgment. They got married a few years after college,and
    the kids came along after that.Grace made her living doing literary criticism,
    and Iain\u2019s books became a hit. He owed it all to her. Without her, the books
    would have been a mess of incoherent ramblings.Grace made everything make sense.\r\n\tTwelve
    years after they got married, Iain took Grace away for their anniversary. She
    wanted to see shows on Broadway. During their second day in New York, Iain wanted
    to see Times Square. Even after Grace assured him he wasn\u2019t missing out
    on anything, they went. The crowd was huge during peak tourist season. They waded
    into the sea of people, but, eventually, Iain lost track of her. He looked everywhere,
    standing on his toes to scan the crowd. I When a hole opened in the crowd, he
    spotted her lying on the pavement. \r\n\tFive years later, Iain is driving the
    kids to visit their mom, who now lies in a cemetery in Denver. He leaves the
    car running and asks the kids to stay put for a little while. He walks up the
    narrow path leading to the cemetery.  He\u2019d walked this  path many times,
    both in and out of his head. He kneels, closes his eyes and talks to her the
    way he\u2019d done  the day she\u2019d dropped that note on his desk. \r\n\tOpening
    his eyes, he looks up to her gravestone. Grace Adrian. Loving Wife and Mother.
    He immediately spots the change \u2014 a new carving in the stone. The last thing
    he remembers before waking up is reading the words: REDACTED.\n"
